<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plegmund</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 923</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plegmund was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 890 to 923, a Mercian scholar recruited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> to help revive learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Alfred names him among those who helped him translate Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoral-care">Pastoral Care</a> into English. As archbishop he reorganised the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> dioceses in the early 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plegmund.jpg" alt="Plegmund" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geni · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Plegmund_Ashmolean.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
